{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 33, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "# **Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\n**Early life and succession**\nRaymond III was born around 1140 as the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father was assassinated by the Hashashin. Raymond III was still a minor at the time of his succession so his mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. He was educated in the court of Jerusalem and became prominent in the politics of the Crusader states.\n\n**Political career and conflicts**\nRaymond III played a major role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a key ally of King Amalric I and later supported Baldwin IV during his reign. Raymond III was also a rival of the Ibelin family and clashed with them over influence in the kingdom. He was captured by Nur ad-Din in 1164 at the Battle of Harim and spent nearly a decade in captivity before being ransomed in 1174.\n\n**Marriage and later years**\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures the Lady of Galilee around 1174. Through this marriage he gained significant influence and land holdings. He served as regent for Baldwin IV from 1174 to 1177 and again for Baldwin V from 1185 to 1186. Raymond III was a central figure during the succession crisis following Baldwin IVs death and the controversial reign of Baldwin V. He opposed Guy of Lusignan and supported Sibylla and her second husband Guy but later switched sides to support Conrad of Montferrat.\n\n**Death and legacy**\nRaymond III died in 1187 without a male heir. His death marked the end of the early Raymond line of Tripoli. He is remembered for his political maneuvering and efforts to maintain stability in the Crusader states during turbulent times. His legacy also includes his support for Conrad of Montferrat and his role in the succession disputes that followed the death of Baldwin V. Raymond IIIs actions had lasting impacts on the politics of Jerusalem and Tripoli in the late 12th century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "#", "logprob": -2.3484303951263428}, {"token": " **", "logprob": -0.8208508491516113}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06473178416490555}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -2.777537883957848e-05}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0013105622492730618}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -9.083335316972807e-05}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -3.3378546504536644e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.19097471237182617}, {"token": "**", "logprob": -0.5195193886756897}, {"token": "Early", "logprob": -0.14976993203163147}, {"token": " life", "logprob": -0.4752733111381531}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.0821232944726944}, {"token": " succession", "logprob": -0.8858807682991028}, {"token": "**\n", "logprob": -0.39802297949790955}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.013223215006291866}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0003912875254172832}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.07697421312332153}, {"token": " born", "logprob": -0.23111209273338318}, {"token": " around", "logprob": -0.24170683324337006}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0008440031087957323}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.6954811548639555e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00020311199477873743}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0010684978915378451}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -0.05538873001933098}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.8732000589370728}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.00019000156316906214}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.1890033632516861}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -5.495397272170521e-05}, {"token": " Raymond", "logprob": -0.0035379454493522644}, {"token": " II", "logprob": -1.4305104514278355e-06}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0060400571674108505}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.0005747812101617455}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -4.2437604861333966e-05}, {"token": " Hod", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token